{"title":"Cauchy's Integral Formula","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Cauchy's Integral Formula</b> is a fundamental result in\n<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_analysis\" title=\"Complex analysis\">complex analysis</a>.  It states that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> is a subset of\nthe complex plane containing a simple counterclockwise loop <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and\nthe region bounded by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is a complex-differentiable function on\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />, then for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> in the interior of the region bounded by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/c/0fc0bf129733da95d5490d106e83bb9ec41f6e1a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2\\pi i} \\int\\limits_C \\frac{f(z)}{z- z_0}dz = f(z_0) .\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"52\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Formula&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> denote the interior of the region bounded by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7ef6667d9d2eec2f214fc632ebfe3d0bdff929e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> denote a simple counterclockwise circle about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />\nof radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />.  Since the interior of the region bounded by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\nis an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Open_set\" title=\"Open set\">open set</a>, there is some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/e/55e04a103e51d5508e80918eb148f90b2cb3f69d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_r \\subset D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" />\nfor all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e86369968e9d631e29a9c299400ee4c0e8fce6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r \\in (0, R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.  For such values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/5/a65e447eab821025d46032bf9c0e95e430ede214.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int\\limits_C \\frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}dz = \\int\\limits_{C_r} \\frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}dz ,\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"53\" />\nby application of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Theorem\" title=\"Cauchy&#39;s Integral Theorem\">Cauchy's Integral Theorem</a>.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is differentiable at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />, for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/f/eaf4418fbe935c15a606516d8f55dc380cd8e822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"7\" height=\"8\" /> we\nmay pick an arbitarily small <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/3/8b3a2616732fe5edb81142978f78b16652df7035.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/2/0b293127845f56818920ae919d21abcdc5c47c53.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left\\lvert \\frac{f(z)-f(z_0)}{z-z_0} - f&#39;(z_0) \\right\\rvert &lt; \\epsilon\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" />\nwhenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/8/d686f184562d00442b971d28e2321afd7da66d42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lvert z - z_0 \\rvert \\le r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />.  Let us parameterize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7ef6667d9d2eec2f214fc632ebfe3d0bdff929e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />\nas <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/2/ab2f960c4d8659caa91f4dcafe8b49c614a4ef2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h(t) = r e^{it}+ z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"123\" height=\"22\" />, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/6/7b6a651be019fda726031d1d4d6a77be1c310c75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t\\in [0,2\\pi]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.   Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/7/f678c1ccd4a118203cce87466cfc28c612529205.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int\\limits_{C_r} f&#39;(z_0)dz = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -28px\" width=\"120\" height=\"52\" /> (again by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Theorem\" title=\"Cauchy&#39;s Integral Theorem\">Cauchy's Integral Theorem</a>), it follows\nthat\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/599f4990cf03981223a526c648954afb7ac58d4e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\biggl\\lvert \\int\\limits_{C_r} \\frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}dz - \\int\\limits_{C_r} \\frac{f(z_0)}{z-z_0}dz \\biggr\\rvert &amp;= \\biggl\\lvert \\int\\limits_{C_r} \\left[ \\frac{f(z) - f(z_0)}{z-z_0} - f&#39;(z_0) \\right] dz \\biggr\\rvert \\\\ &amp;\\le \\int\\limits_0^{2\\pi} \\left\\lvert \\frac{f(h(t)) - f(z_0)}{ h(t) - z_0} - f&#39;(z_0) \\right\\vert \\cdot r dt \\\\ &amp;&lt; \\int\\limits_0^{2\\pi} \\epsilon \\cdot r dt = 2\\pi \\epsilon r . \\end{align*}\" width=\"498\" height=\"197\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/f/eaf4418fbe935c15a606516d8f55dc380cd8e822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\epsilon$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"7\" height=\"8\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> can simultaneously become arbitrarily\nsmall, it follows that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/9/dd9c1e28f7505efc2e8176323bfc27901c5dfb95.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\int\\limits_C \\frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}dz &amp;= \\int\\limits_{C_r} \\frac{f(z_0)} {z- z_0}dz \\\\ &amp;= f(z_0) \\int\\limits_{0}^{2\\pi} \\frac{h&#39;(t)}{h(t) - z_0} dt \\\\ &amp;= f(z_0) \\int\\limits_{0}^{2\\pi} \\frac{ir e^{it}}{re^{it}} dt \\\\ &amp;= f(z_0) \\cdot 2\\pi i , \\end{align*}\" width=\"268\" height=\"223\" />\nwhich is equivalent to the desired theorem.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Consequences\">Consequences</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Formula&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Consequences\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By induction, we see that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th derivative of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/9/bc9d36fc69e5cc3cfbf69c8ce8feeef1750aa469.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f^{(n)}(z_0) = \\frac{n!}{2\\pi i} \\int\\limits_C \\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}dz,\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"52\" />\nfor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/0247db3d702b5ce6be98f50771750a2723cddcea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.  In particular, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th derivative <i>exists</i> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />,\nfor all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/0247db3d702b5ce6be98f50771750a2723cddcea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.  In other words, if a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is\ncomplex-differentiable on some region, then it is <i>infinitely</i>\ndifferentiable<i> on the interior of that region.</i>\n</p><p>Since the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/c/99cb3f3a8b3bbfc702c5863d16602a06cded535d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />th derivative exists in general, it follows that\nthe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th derivative is continuous.  This is not true for functions\nof real variables!  For instance the real function\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/5/735451baa3462a013e891717484339062009a5ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x) = \\begin{cases} x \\sin(1/x), &amp; x \\neq 0 \\\\ 0, &amp; x=0 \\end{cases}\\]\" width=\"216\" height=\"53\" />\nis everywhere differentiable, but its derivative is mysteriously\nnot continuous at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ea5b6f6de584d56d19a89cc4923a9d9a5cfa41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.  In complex analysis, the mystery disappears:\nthe function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/5/205120f9699a56bb22fccbc3bb02315be1467c98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\sin(1/z) = z\\frac{e^{i/z} - e^{-i/z}}{2i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"199\" height=\"40\" /> has an\n<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Essential_singularity&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Essential singularity (page does not exist)\">essential singularity</a> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/0/72079803c6fcdcfa95b6d7f383f6ad71f1eb631b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=0$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />, so we can't establish a derivative\nthere in any case.\n</p><p>The theorem is useful for estimating a function (or its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th derivative)\nat a point based on the behavior of the function around the point.\nFor instance, the theorem yields an easy proof that <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Holomorphic_function\" title=\"Holomorphic function\">holomorphic functions</a>\nare in fact <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Analytic_function&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Analytic function (page does not exist)\"> analytic</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Formula&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy%27s_Integral_Theorem\" title=\"Cauchy&#39;s Integral Theorem\">Cauchy's Integral Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018075915\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.022 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.034 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 266/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9605/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8011:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018075915 and revision id 211137. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}